{"title":"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 15","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><dl><dd><i>The following problem is from the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_19\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 19\">2025 AMC 10B #19</a> and <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">2025 AMC 12B #15</a>, so those problems redirect to this page.</i></dd></dl>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(2_min)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (2 min)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Calculus)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Calculus)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Construction)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Construction)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5(Similar_to_Solution_1)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5(Similar to Solution 1)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_FormulaFocus_(Logical,_Good_Editing,_~0:50)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by FormulaFocus (Logical, Good Editing, ~0:50)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution(Quick_and_Simple)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution(Quick and Simple)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_(Fast_and_Easy)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution (Fast and Easy)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_SpreadTheMathLove\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by SpreadTheMathLove</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_MadMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by MadMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A container has a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/8/8a8df7312744a199daa5c8008af5b9b4798bfd64.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1\\times 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"39\" height=\"12\" /> square bottom, a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/5/7b5343f2d9e22816e8de24262565ff2937695b2c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\times 3$\" width=\"40\" height=\"12\" /> open square top, and four congruent trapezoidal sides, as shown. Starting when the container is empty, a hose that runs water at a constant rate takes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7f6c79f2148f0cec592c9517b40ee1b82ef22e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$35$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> minutes to fill the container up to the midline of the trapezoids. \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/b/cdb9ac6f58025e389fb6367673d69b0cd873cba5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] /* Made by Mathemagician108 */ import graph3; size(200); real a = 1; real d = 0.6; real h = 0.9; triple A1 = (a,0,0); triple B1 = (0,a,0); triple C1 = (-a,0,0); triple D1 = (0,-a,0); triple A2 = (a+d,0,h); triple B2 = (0,a+d,h); triple C2 = (-(a+d),0,h); triple D2 = (0,-(a+d),h); triple A3 = (a+2*d,0,2*h); triple B3 = (0,a+2*d,2*h); triple C3 = (-(a+2*d),0,2*h); triple D3 = (0,-(a+2*d),2*h);  path3 bottom = A1--B1--C1--D1--cycle; path3 backright = A1--B1--B2--A2--cycle; path3 backleft = B1--C1--C2--B2--cycle; path3 frontleft = C1--D1--D3--C3--cycle; path3 frontright = D1--A1--A3--D3--cycle;  defaultpen(1); pen blue = rgb(202, 201, 255); pen red = rgb(255, 164, 164);  // colored surfaces draw(surface(bottom), emissive(gray)); draw(surface(backright), emissive(blue)); draw(surface(backleft), emissive(blue)); draw(surface(frontleft), emissive(red)); draw(surface(frontright), emissive(red)); // black outlines draw(A1--D1--C1); draw(A2--D2--C2); draw(A3--D3--C3, linewidth(1.4)); draw((0.32,0.68,0)--B1--(-0.32,0.68,0)); draw((1.25,0.35,h)--B2--(-1.25,0.35,h)); draw(A3--B3--C3); draw(A1--A3); draw(B1--B3); draw(C1--C3); draw(D1--D3, linewidth(1.4));  currentprojection=perspective((0,-4,3)); [/asy]\" width=\"335\" height=\"208\" />\n</p><p>How many more minutes will it take to fill the remainder of the container?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/9/e293375335bb8cc3f07e4f9493421974650f5652.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A) }70 \\qquad \\text{(B) }85 \\qquad \\text{(C) }90 \\qquad \\text{(D) }95 \\qquad \\text{(E) }105$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"387\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Extend the edges pointing downwards to converge at a point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> to form a square pyramid. Consider 3 square pyramids, the large one formed by the top vertices of the original figure and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, the middle one formed by the medians running through the sides of the original figure and point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, and the smaller one formed by the bottom vertices of the original figure and point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />. Note that all pyramids are similar since they are all essentially scaled by a certain factor.\n</p><p>The median length is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/b/ccb042aeae097441ea2bb11a28fef41cd8621edf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3+1}{2}=2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"80\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>Using side length to volume ratios, find that the volumes must have ratios <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/6/f362522dcb62f7076ba2343082469e35c578a363.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1:8:27$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"68\" height=\"14\" />\nThen, you get that the ratio of the volume thus filled to the volume that we must fill is equivalent to \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/3/7736f550cc7e4fa3c8b0403e5c4d1e9cd74b3e02.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8-1:27-8 =  7:19$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"174\" height=\"15\" />.\nThus, it will take \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/2/122ffc4ac5aef8431d159fd0ddf97812a9b78286.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{19}{7}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"24\" height=\"38\" /> \nmore time to fill the remaining volume giving us an answer of \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/8/04868b6b467cd70a5c235961d89e4366e670373b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{19}{7} * 35 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(D) }95}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"149\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>-Failure.net\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.282_min.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(2_min)\">Solution 2 (2 min)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (2 min)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Note that dividing this shape along the midline gives two truncated pyramids with base areas \\(1\\) and \\(4\\), and \\(4\\) and \\(9\\) (a truncated pyramid is a pyramid is cut at some point parallel to the base to reach a shape with bases that are squares). Using the truncated pyramid volume formula\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/7/d07fd26656fa45f1ff45d6fc9fa7b4aa95706283.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[V = \\frac{1}{3}h\\,(A_1 + A_2 + \\sqrt{A_1A_2}),\\]\" width=\"229\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>we get volumes\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/7/d6724433ae9f0807997dda0215ea4ba51041ce5b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{3}h(1 + 4 + \\sqrt{4}) \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\frac{1}{3}h(4 + 9 + \\sqrt{36}).\\]\" width=\"353\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>Writing their ratio, we obtain\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36fb6fc56f53ee8b753464ca8953d08d50414d36.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{13 + 6}{5 + 2} = \\frac{19}{7}.\\]\" width=\"101\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>Thus,\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/3/59324b64a6b540e87128d01210fbec8f112f817b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[35 \\cdot \\frac{19}{7} = 95.\\]\" width=\"98\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>So the remaining time is \\(\\boxed{95}\\).\n</p><p>-Cyrus825\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Calculus.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Calculus)\">Solution 3 (Calculus)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Calculus)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The volume that was filled in 35 minutes can be represented as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/e/64e8aa0a387cde0eb4ba7af14a1eb672e0b45dd7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\int_1^2 s^2 \\text{d}s = \\frac{1}{3}\\left(2^3 - 1^3\\right) = \\frac{7}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"213\" height=\"46\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> represents the side length of the horizontal cross-sectional square. Then, the volume that remains to be filled is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/6/256db9150bd39647ab464455c0880b8d55def619.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\int_2^3 s^2 \\text{d}s = \\frac{1}{3}\\left(3^3 - 2^3\\right) = \\frac{19}{3}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"227\" height=\"46\" /> Since the filling rate is the same, the amount of time this will take is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/5/415ec2a25aac3393e4a248a8efec4f6787d1708d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$35 \\cdot \\frac{3}{7} \\cdot \\frac{19}{3} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(D) }95}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"171\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>-grape1984\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Construction.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Construction)\">Solution 4 (Construction)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Construction)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Extend the bottom of the container to form an upside down square pyramid. Then take a diagonal cross section as shown below.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/2/9c27afdc77b70f06f6ccd21362b8cc75523b30f9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(220);  // Outer (largest) upside-down isosceles triangle pair A = (0,-4);      // bottom vertex pair B = (-3,0);      // left base point pair C = (3,0);       // right base point  draw(A--B--C--cycle);  // Middle triangle (scaled toward A) real k2 = 0.6; pair B2 = A + k2*(B - A); pair C2 = A + k2*(C - A); draw(A--B2--C2--cycle);  // Smallest triangle (scaled toward A) real k3 = 0.3; pair B3 = A + k3*(B - A); pair C3 = A + k3*(C - A); draw(A--B3--C3--cycle);  // --- Heights (altitudes) ---  // Outer triangle height pair mid1 = (B + C)/2; draw(mid1--A); label(&quot;$x_1$&quot;, midpoint(mid1--A)+(0.25,1.25));   // centered on the segment label(&quot;$3\\sqrt{2}$&quot;, midpoint(mid1--A)+(3.4,2));   // centered on the segment  // Middle triangle height pair mid2 = (B2 + C2)/2; draw(mid2--A); label(&quot;$x_2$&quot;, midpoint(mid2--A)+(0.25,0.65));   // centered on the segment label(&quot;$2\\sqrt{2}$&quot;, midpoint(mid2--A)+(2.3,1.2));   // centered on the segment  // Smallest triangle height pair mid3 = (B3 + C3)/2; draw(mid3--A); label(&quot;$x_3$&quot;, midpoint(mid3--A)+(0.25,0.25));   // centered on the segment label(&quot;$\\sqrt{2}$&quot;, midpoint(mid3--A)+(1.2,0.6));   // centered on the segment [/asy]\" width=\"368\" height=\"232\" />\n</p><p>You can notice that there are several similar triangles, so we write several equations.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/6/c9688374649372eb1cddf7e8aaf353a85703c222.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{x_3}{x_1+x_2+x_3}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\]\" width=\"160\" height=\"45\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/0/490d9fb3ebad671657cc6c0de90d17a002f84477.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{x_3}{x_2+x_3}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\]\" width=\"120\" height=\"45\" />\n</p><p>Then solve the system to get: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/6/e963eccf98294ff1859bf8d3efecd664cbf01868.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x_1=x_2=x_3=x\\]\" width=\"136\" height=\"11\" />\n</p><p>Write the volumes of the three pyramids with sides of the bases 1,2, and 3 respectively:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/7/9b790bc0cfa0f720ea681b33a1d8270c737e3541.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[V_1=\\frac{x}{3}\\]\" width=\"54\" height=\"32\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/0/11013e0d2d40db7e378dce319b196ace4a5a190f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[V_2=\\frac{8x}{3}\\]\" width=\"63\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/4/b04e11285d3a974f7fc556158df4b38cd63c5f06.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[V_3=9x\\]\" width=\"60\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p>The volumes of the part of the container below the midline and above it are.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/d/16d03a8f349d052878addee54ac754eca4e3d8cf.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[V_2-V_1=\\frac{7x}{3}\\]\" width=\"103\" height=\"38\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/e/b3e7dcf6b09c3bbacb4c6155219d669f648f2242.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[V_3-V_2=\\frac{19x}{3}\\]\" width=\"112\" height=\"37\" /> \n</p><p>Then the time <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/d/e0d2bf360290fd61d1c1557e763f2622363b3d35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"13\" /> for the top to fill up is given by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/5/00573d34667d7a509e5954286dc1efb9693df854.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{\\frac{19x}{3}}{\\frac{7x}{3}}=\\frac{t}{35}\\]\" width=\"71\" height=\"47\" /> \nTherefore  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/9/2a90589782c4e41a7a9e9acc1891648739a26ca3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t=\\boxed{\\mathbf{(D)}\\ 95}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"98\" height=\"32\" />\n</p><p>-AARONPICK2\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5.28Similar_to_Solution_1.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5(Similar_to_Solution_1)\">Solution 5(Similar to Solution 1)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5(Similar to Solution 1)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Extend the trapezoidal shape into a square pyramid. Note that we're only extending the height by another factor of the height. In other words, if the height from the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/9/3b931b4ecb324b5389d91919ee30d53b913cd5b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\times 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"39\" height=\"12\" /> base to the midline is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/8/8189a5b5a0917b8c93350827be4038af1839139d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$h$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />, the height from the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/c/e4c044326e2e84b768dafef07060bd42b0a5cb21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\times 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> base(which is the midline) to the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/7/d278803d72fcc493afb1559174a483aa7f41d143.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\times 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> base(which is the finish) is also <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/8/8189a5b5a0917b8c93350827be4038af1839139d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$h$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />. Now using this logic, the distance from the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/9/3b931b4ecb324b5389d91919ee30d53b913cd5b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\times 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"39\" height=\"12\" /> base to the top of the pyramid is obviously also <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/8/8189a5b5a0917b8c93350827be4038af1839139d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$h$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />. Now the problem is easy. Computing the volume of the first half of the trapezoidal shape(which takes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7f6c79f2148f0cec592c9517b40ee1b82ef22e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$35$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> minutes for the hose to fill) is \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/4/544b7e6e01707071031f33ccf594d564e2cc61ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3} (2 \\cdot 2) \\cdot (2h) - \\frac{1}{3} (1 \\cdot 1) \\cdot (h) = \\frac{7h}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"273\" height=\"39\" /> which takes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7f6c79f2148f0cec592c9517b40ee1b82ef22e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$35$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> minutes. So to fill up to the height <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/8/8189a5b5a0917b8c93350827be4038af1839139d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$h$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> we get\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/8/8189a5b5a0917b8c93350827be4038af1839139d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$h$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> takes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/c/d5caa2182f899c2f0f79a28260c20d8a62128f53.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$35 \\cdot \\frac{3}{7} = 15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"89\" height=\"38\" /> minutes to fill. \n</p><p>Now to find the volume of the last half of he trapezoidal shape we get\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/1/b11480dd34c5e777d88eeeced46b1728d93a0f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3} (3 \\cdot 3) \\cdot (3h) - \\frac{1}{3} (2 \\cdot 2) \\cdot (2h) = \\frac{19h}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"292\" height=\"39\" /> so if we plug in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/8/8189a5b5a0917b8c93350827be4038af1839139d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$h$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> takes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/9/f1965fae079a9ba2c0726c307070c2355dfcb213.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> minutes to fill we get\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/6/9367c6568f87e2e407b83f1a2509e0125fcaf523.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{19h}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"34\" height=\"39\" /> takes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/6/606c9cc92eab55ca39721f473096830c9e3fb4f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{19 \\cdot 15}{3} = 19 \\cdot 5 = \\boxed{95}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"174\" height=\"38\" /> minutes to fill.\n</p><p>~ilikemath247365\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_FormulaFocus_.28Logical.2C_Good_Editing.2C_.7E0:50.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_FormulaFocus_(Logical,_Good_Editing,_~0:50)\">Video Solution by FormulaFocus (Logical, Good Editing, ~0:50)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by FormulaFocus (Logical, Good Editing, ~0:50)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shorts/S1mFur6p64U\">https://www.youtube.com/shorts/S1mFur6p64U</a>\n</p><p>~Ebai\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution.28Quick_and_Simple.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution(Quick_and_Simple)\">Video Solution(Quick and Simple)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution(Quick and Simple)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9jhhfzPr8IQ\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9jhhfzPr8IQ</a>\n~Sigma_Bob\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_.28Fast_and_Easy.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_(Fast_and_Easy)\">Video Solution (Fast and Easy)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution (Fast and Easy)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S5_iQpIN43w\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S5_iQpIN43w</a>\n</p><p>~ Pi Academy\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_SpreadTheMathLove\">Video Solution by SpreadTheMathLove</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?titl